    url = {https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2407929121},
    issn = {0027-8424},
    abstract = {Significance We established a strain learning mechanical metamaterial that can not only recover after plastic deformation but also become stronger and stiffer in response to the applied loads. We demonstrate that shape-memory protein–polymer networks can unravel to release their stored length and, after recovery, will reconfigure to accommodate higher loads. These materials are incorporated into lattice frameworks using additive manufacturing which are then shown to become up to 2.5× stiffer after being crushed to 80% strain and recovered. This strain learning behavior enables the creation of metamaterials that are “teachable” and can autonomously remodel in response to applied loads, similar to the processes that occur in natural materials.}
}

